     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The current FDA-approved label for amlodipine (Norvasc) lists a maximum of 10 mg once daily for adults. It doesn't allow 50 mg, so the dose is well outside labeled dosing. An overdose at this level can cause profound hypotension and shock that last a long time. I can't enter it on the basis that it's within the label.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a labeled dose.** Give me 2.5, 5, or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it now.\n- **Verify the label.** You can check the dosing section of the current prescribing information. I expect it will show 10 mg as the maximum.\n- **Talk to pharmacy.** If you have a specific reason to go above the labeled range, a pharmacist or the attending can review it and place or co-sign the order through the usual override process.\n- **Consider add-on therapy.** If 10 mg isn't controlling blood pressure, adding a second agent from a different class is the standard approach.\n\nI'm glad to help with any of these."
      }
     ],
